    "id": "thm:effective-zero-allowed-compiler",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Put \\(e=N(N-1)\\), \\(q=|\\mathcal I|\\), \\(p=nN\\), and let \\(\\lambda\\ge1\\) bound the rational input bit length.  The downward-closed cover in either direction is decidable by the existing affine-chart compiler after finite disjunction over exact subgraphs.  With\n\\[\n s_{\\le}=(q+N+1)2^{9N^2},\\qquad d_{\\le}=4N^3,\n \\qquad \\tau_{\\le}=(\\lambda+1)2^{9N^2},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n \\mathsf B_{\\le}\n = (\\tau_{\\le}+1)^2(s_{\\le}+1)^{(p+1)(p+N+1)+1}\n (d_{\\le}+1)^{c_{\\mathrm{qe}}(p+1)(p+N+1)},                         \\tag{21}\n\\]\none directed downward-closed decision takes at most \\(2^e\\mathsf B_{\\le}\\) bit operations, comparison takes at most \\(2^{e+1}\\mathsf B_{\\le}\\), and enumeration of the zero-allowed equivalence class modulo latent relabeling takes at most\n\\[\n 2^e\\bigl(2^{e+1}\\mathsf B_{\\le}+\\ell!\\,N^2\\bigr)                  \\tag{22}\n\\]\nbit operations, apart from fixed linear encoding overhead.  The algorithm returns finite sign-cell certificates and contains no coefficient or source-law quantifier.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "ell",
      "N",
      "Ical",
      "DownCover"
    ],
