    ],
    "proof_tex": "For a fixed \\(F\\subseteq G\\), replace the target-domain clause in (14) by the finite disjunction over \\(K\\subseteq H\\):\n\\[\n \\forall C\\left[\\operatorname{Dom}_F(C)\\Longrightarrow\n \\bigvee_{K\\subseteq H}\\ \\bigvee_{U,W,f}\n \\exists(D,a)\\ \\Theta_{K,f}(C,D,a)\\right].                          \\tag{23}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\Theta_{K,f}\\) is the explicit conjunction (12)--(13) with \\(\\operatorname{Dom}_K(D)\\).  By the definition of downward cover and the zero-allowed coupled-equivalence theorem, all formulas (23), one for each \\(F\\subseteq G\\), hold exactly when the directed zero-allowed containment holds.  Thus (23) is already free of structural coefficients and source distributions before elimination.\n\nThere are at most \\(2^e<2^{N^2}\\) choices of \\(K\\).  Multiplying the exact compiler's conservative number of atoms by this number gives at most\n\\[\n (q+N+1)2^{8N^2+N^2}=s_{\\le}.\n\\]\nThe added operation is a Boolean disjunction, so the degree remains at most \\(d_{\\le}=4N^3\\).  Encoding all copied atoms is bounded by the deliberately larger coefficient bit length \\(\\tau_{\\le}\\).  The quantified real-variable blocks remain \\(C\\) of size \\(p\\), and \\((D,a)\\) of size at most \\(p+N\\), because \\(K,U,W,f\\) are finite discrete choices rather than real variables.  The cited effective elimination lemma therefore gives the bound \\(\\mathsf B_{\\le}\\) in (21) for each fixed \\(F\\).\n\nAt most \\(2^e\\) subgraphs \\(F\\) are checked, proving the directed bound.  Two directions prove the comparison bound.  Finally test the \\(2^e\\) labeled candidate graphs and canonicalize each under at most \\(\\ell!\\) latent permutations, at \\(N^2\\) bit comparisons apiece, to obtain (22).  Every loop is finite, and the sign-cell output retains zero signs and all lower-dimensional strata, so the algorithm terminates with a checkable exact certificate.",
